<p>These remedies are not mutually exclusive, though a court will take into account amounts already awarded or paid under another proceeding so that there is no duplication. Where matrimonial proceedings are already pending, Section 24 is often the swiftest route to an interim figure. Where no such proceeding exists, the summary jurisdiction is generally the appropriate starting point.</p>

<h2>The stages of the proceeding</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>1. Filing</h3><p>The petition or application is filed before the Family Court having territorial jurisdiction, accompanied by the affidavit of assets, income and expenditure, and the supporting documents.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2. Notice and appearance</h3><p>Notice issues to the respondent, who enters appearance and is likewise required to file his affidavit of assets, income and expenditure.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>3. Interim maintenance</h3><p>An application for interim maintenance is generally taken up early. It is decided on affidavits and documents, without trial, and a figure is fixed to run during the pendency of the proceeding.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>4. Reply and rejoinder</h3><p>The respondent files his reply to the main application; the petitioner files a rejoinder where necessary.</p></div>
