  <div class="fstep"><h3>6. Arguments and final order</h3><p>The court fixes the quantum, the date from which it runs, and the mode and date of payment each month.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>7. Execution or enforcement</h3><p>Where the order is not complied with, recovery is pursued through the enforcement machinery provided by the relevant statute.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The affidavit of assets, income and expenditure</h2>

<p>This document does more work than any other in a maintenance proceeding. It requires each party to disclose income from all sources, assets held, liabilities and monthly expenditure, and it is filed on oath. Courts across the country now require it as a matter of course at the threshold.</p>

<p>Two errors recur. The first is a disclosure that is technically true but materially incomplete — a declared salary that omits allowances, rental income, agricultural income or business receipts. The second is an expenditure statement that does not sit with the assets declared, where a person claiming a modest income accounts for expenses that plainly exceed it. Where the disclosure is found unreliable, the court is entitled to look past declared income to earning capacity, and to assess maintenance on that basis. A carefully prepared affidavit protects the party filing it; a careless one becomes the strongest material in the hands of the other side.</p>

<h2>Documents worth assembling early</h2>

<div class="check">
<p>Income tax returns and Form 16 for the preceding three years, together with Form 26AS where available.</p>
<p>Bank statements for all accounts for at least the preceding year, and credit card statements, which frequently reveal a standard of living that the declared income does not explain.</p>
<p>Salary slips, appointment letters and, in the case of the self-employed, the balance sheet, GST returns and details of the business.</p>
<p>Documents of title or possession relating to immovable property, and details of vehicles, investments and insurance.</p>
<p>Evidence of the standard of living during cohabitation — photographs, travel records, school fee receipts, records of the matrimonial home — which bears directly on quantum.</p>
<p>Where children are involved, school fee structures, records of medical expenditure and details of any special needs.</p>
</div>

<h2>How quantum is approached</h2>

<p>There is no formula. Courts weigh the status of the parties, the reasonable needs of the claimant, the income and earning capacity of the respondent, his own liabilities and dependants, the number of persons he must maintain, and the standard of living to which the claimant was accustomed during cohabitation. The object is that the claimant should be able to live in a manner reasonably comparable to that standard, not merely to subsist.</p>

<p>Where the claimant is herself earning, that is relevant but not determinative. The question is whether her income is sufficient to maintain the standard of living in question, not simply whether she has any income at all. Similarly, a respondent's assertion of unemployment is examined against his qualifications, his history of earning and his assets, since earning capacity rather than present idleness is the touchstone.</p>

<h2>The date from which maintenance runs</h2>

<p>This is frequently overlooked and frequently consequential. The court may direct that maintenance run from the date of the application or from the date of the order, and it must give reasons. Where a proceeding has lasted four or five years, the difference between those two dates can be substantial. The prayer clause should ask specifically for maintenance from the date of the application, and the pleading should set out why — for instance, the period for which the claimant has been without support, or delays in the proceeding not attributable to her.</p>

<h2>Enforcement</h2>

<p>An order that is not obeyed is worth little, and the enforcement provisions are therefore part of the plan from the outset. Under the summary jurisdiction, arrears may be recovered in the manner provided for the levy of fines, and the court has power to direct imprisonment for each month's default in appropriate cases, though this is a mode of enforcement and not a discharge of the liability. Orders under the matrimonial statutes are enforced as decrees. In practice, orders that specify the mode of payment — direct bank transfer to a nominated account, by a fixed date each month — are markedly easier to enforce than orders which leave the mode open, and it is worth asking for that specificity when the order is being framed.</p>

<h2>A note on timelines</h2>

<p>Interim maintenance is generally decided within a few months of filing, though this varies with the court's docket and the conduct of the parties. Final adjudication takes considerably longer, since it requires evidence and cross-examination on both sides. Realistic expectations at the outset make for better decisions along the way — particularly the decision whether to press for an early interim order and let the final adjudication take its course, or to seek an expedited trial.</p>

<p>This note is a general description of procedure and is not advice on any particular case. The appropriate course in a given matter depends on its own facts, the relationship between the parties, and the proceedings already pending between them.</p>
